00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.180
We'll have Scott now touch on
how does the demo prevention

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.000
service, to begin with, offer
this kind of protection,

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.500
where different options are.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:26.430
And once Scott is done, we
will come back to Apigee the

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.490
and show you how you can easily
[INAUDIBLE] the data loss

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:35.640
prevention service and secure
sensitive data within an API.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.168
With that, Scott.

00:12:37.168 --> 00:12:37.960
SCOTT ELLIS: Great.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.210
Thanks, Prithpal.

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:42.960
And I think you showed how the
Apigee extensions can really

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.810
make it easy for
administrators and developers

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:49.380
to both deploy APIs and
have that enterprise grade

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.270
control over your API.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.780
So we're going to talk about
how the DLP Extension can give

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.990
the same admins and developers
a level of data protection

00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.360
across other APIs.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.320
But before we do that,
let's talk a little bit

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.340
deeper on what our Cloud
DLP can actually provide.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:06.840
So what we're going
to do is, first,

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:08.010
let's just kind of
dive in a little bit

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.560
and understand what sensitive
data is and the kind of things

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.250
that we're going
to protect against.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.850
So just some basic
examples of sensitive data.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:16.800
If you heard things like
personal identifiable

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.080
information, or PII,
it might be related

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.110
to financial data,
health data, and it could

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.850
come from a variety of sources.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.290
It could come from your users.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:27.018
It could come about
your employees,

00:13:27.018 --> 00:13:28.560
or it could also be
something that is

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.050
shared between your partners.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:33.870
And, so, sensitive data can
enter into your enterprise

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.050
in a lot of different ways.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.510
And as you're
building your APIs,

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.000
since you're interacting
with all of these sources,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.610
we want to show you how you
can protect and understand

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.110
where this sensitive
data is slowing

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:44.490
throughout your enterprise.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.740
And really the goal that
you have when you're

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.240
dealing with sensitive data--

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.350
we kind of summarized
into two goals here.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:52.530
One is you want to use
that data properly,

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.120
and you want to
minimize exposure.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.940
You can relate this to some
concepts around least privilege

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.920
or need to know,
but the basic idea

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:02.490
is if you don't need a bit of
sensitive data to do something,

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:04.080
then you don't want
to collect that

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.970
or you don't want
to expose that.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:09.755
The second part here is
how do you be accountable?

00:14:09.755 --> 00:14:11.130
And the key thing
here is you can

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.287
have a policy that says
how to use data properly,

00:14:14.287 --> 00:14:16.620
but you really also want to
be able to demonstrate that.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.590
And so will you be accountable
from an audit perspective

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.700
or just from a best
practices perspective.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:25.600
And so, really, these controls
will help you do both of these.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.960
So how can Cloud Data
Loss Prevention help?

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.000
What Cloud Data Loss
Prevention brings

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.010
is the ability to
discover and classify

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:37.210
sensitive data, as well as
de-identify or mask that data.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.570
And so out of the box when
we talk about discovery

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:41.880
and classification what
we're talking about

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.730
is the ability to go in and
look for things like credit card

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.450
numbers, social security
numbers, different types of PII

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.730
that maybe you've
had global types that

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.230
could be country specific.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.900
And so out of the box our
Cloud DLP Infrastructure

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.090
has about 90
built-in classifiers.

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.400
And then you can go in
and create your own custom

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.400
classifiers or augment
the ones that we have.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.310
Once you find the data, the
next thing you might want to do

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:07.890
is actually go in and figure
out how to secure the data.

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:10.350
And this is a process known
as de-identification or data

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:11.280
masking.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:14.520
And so the idea here is, let's
say you find a credit card

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.600
number in a particular set of
text or a request a response

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:19.800
coming into your API.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.930
You want to actually potentially
go in and either just redact

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.620
that out or mask it somehow.

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.660
The animation that
we're showing here

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.430
is showing a little
bit of a before

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.290
and after on some
structured data.

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.030
And we're just showing
the application

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:34.530
of a variety of
different techniques

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.320
here, things like
format-preserving encryption

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.390
where we can actually
take an identifier

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.330
and produce another identifier
that obscures the data

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.880
but still keeps a lot of the
integrity of the original data,

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.910
as well as doing
different types of masking

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:49.210
or partial masking.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.498
In that last, column
the Comment field,

00:15:51.498 --> 00:15:53.290
we're showing something
very powerful here.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:55.890
We're actually combining our
inspection and our masking

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.440
together to actually
go into the text here.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:01.170
And if you notice in
the Comment field,

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.090
we're going in and
looking for sensitive data

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:05.550
and only masking that
part of the Comment deal

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:07.210
that has a sensitive data.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:09.377
And that's something that
we'll show an example here

00:16:09.377 --> 00:16:10.870
to an API scenario.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:14.140
So how can this help you with
your production workload?

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:16.312
So let's look at a
example scenario.

00:16:16.312 --> 00:16:18.270
Let's say that you have
a customer support chat

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.180
application and in
that chat application

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.550
you have a customer
and a support agent

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:25.560
just communicating
back and forth.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.740
And in the context of that
chat the customer decides

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.530
to overshare, they decide
to give more information

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:32.590
than you actually need.

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:34.800
So in this case, we are
looking for the last four

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.185
digits of their social to
validate their account.

00:16:37.185 --> 00:16:40.012
But the customer gave their
full social, their phone number,

00:16:40.012 --> 00:16:42.220
their email address, just
gave a lot more information

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:44.100
than the support agent wanted.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:46.960
And we may not want to
collect that information.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:49.270
We may not even want to
show it to the agent.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:52.350
So by using our inspection
and masking tools

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:55.530
we can actually go in and
mask that data in real time,

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:58.140
either before the agent
saw it or before we

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.980
persisted to this, depending
on what you want to do there.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:03.240
And so this is a very
high-level scenario here.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.910
And next we're going
to go in and show

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:10.300
a little bit deeper examples
of how this might work on APIs.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:13.510
So, really, with
Apigee and Cloud DLP,

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.890
how can we give your
enterprise that data protection

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.210
across your API?

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:21.210
So admins can apply this
from an admin perspective,

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.113
or developers can
take advantage of it

00:17:23.113 --> 00:17:25.470
as easy as how Prithpal
showed earlier.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:28.530
Just applying an extension
and deploying this.

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:30.730
Let's walk through
a couple examples.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:35.040
So, first, imagine how
your enterprise is working.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.140
You've got clients at
the top here coming

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:38.880
from a variety of
different sources.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.290
Behind the scenes,
you've got all

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.450
of your actual services,
your stores, everything

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:43.590
going on there.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.770
And the arrows represent
various interactions

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.765
between your backend
systems and your clients.

00:17:49.765 --> 00:17:51.390
And what we just want
to highlight here

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.827
is this red arrow.

00:17:53.827 --> 00:17:55.410
Let's say it contained
sensitive data,

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:58.410
and it's some sort of sensitive
data going out to someone

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:00.000
on the web, a user on the web.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.560
It could be a potential
data exposure.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.375
And so, again,
sensitive data going out

00:18:04.375 --> 00:18:06.750
would be something where you
might be revealing something

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:10.260
from an internal system
and exposing it externally.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.440
Whereas something coming in on
a request, the second red arrow

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:18.030
here, where maybe a partner is
sending information to your API

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:19.440
that you don't
intend to collect.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:20.790
Maybe it has sensitive
data in there

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.165
that you don't want to log,
or you don't want to collect.

00:18:23.165 --> 00:18:27.180
And that could be an example
of a potential inadvertent data

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:28.080
collection.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.570
And so when you think of
things like data minimization

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.220
and least privilege
and need to know,

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:36.420
you really want to look at how
can I produce these exposures,

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:41.040
as well as reduce collecting
information that we don't need.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.250
And that's where the Apigee
API Platform with the Edge

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:46.530
extension and Cloud
DLP can really

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:50.190
help you inspect and mask
across all of your APIs.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.670
So with the arrows now
that are showing in green,

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:56.500
we're basically saying, we
can actually apply that policy

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.380
so that you are only
exposing and collecting

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.420
the information
that you intend to.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:03.970
Just walk through just a
high-level example of these.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:07.020
So let's say that you
have an order API.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.620
In this order API, the
intent is that the requester

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.230
is going to send in an
order ID, and then you're

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:16.080
going to respond back with a
status, maybe a transaction

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:16.980
amount.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.990
But in this example that's
highlighted in the red box,

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:24.000
we have a user of the API decide
they're going to also include

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.980
an email address and
a raw credit card

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:27.540
number in the request.

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:30.720
Now, this isn't something you
need to process the request,

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.840
but it may be something that you
just accept and end up logging

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.880
because you logged the
request that came in.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.250
And even though you don't
need or use that information,

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.660
you really don't want to log it.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:41.868
And you shouldn't be collecting
it because that's not

00:19:41.868 --> 00:19:43.290
the purpose of this API.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:46.350
So when we talk about
inspecting and then

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:49.440
applying DLP across our API,
what we're really saying

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.420
is you don't want
this information.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:55.260
And with DLP, we can go in and
do something like masking it.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:58.562
So now when this request comes
in, the order ID is preserved,

00:19:58.562 --> 00:20:00.270
but the email address
and the card number

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:01.710
are just automatically
masked out

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.960
of the request parameter
because you didn't need them.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.592
So now when you log
this information,

00:20:05.592 --> 00:20:07.050
you're still going
to see that this

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.660
as partner or this requester
sent that information.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.880
You're just not logging the
actual raw sensitive PII.

00:20:14.658 --> 00:20:16.200
You were showing
the redaction there,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.510
but you really have the
full power of our Cloud DLP

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:19.630
infrastructure.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:22.470
So it may be that you want
to collect that information.

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:24.060
You don't want to
just redact it,

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:26.260
but you want to do something
even more powerful.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:28.410
So let's look at that
similar example here.

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:30.720
But instead of just simply
redacting the information,

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.660
because you have the full
power of the tokenization

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:36.240
and masking, you
could actually go in

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:39.600
and use tokenization
methods to obscure that data

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.340
but still keep the integrity.

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.910
And what we mean here
is that that long token

00:20:43.910 --> 00:20:46.410
that you see there for the email
address and the credit card

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:49.590
number keep integrity because
if multiple requests are coming

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.303
in with the same email or
credit card number being sent,

00:20:52.303 --> 00:20:54.720
you actually can still join
and match on that information.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.240
You keep the integrity
there, but your APIs

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:02.220
not logging or collecting
or preserving that raw PII.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:03.880
You're using a token instead.

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:06.380
So that's a very powerful
thing that you can do,

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.510
which is as simple as changing
the configuration on the DLP

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:10.367
side.

00:21:10.367 --> 00:21:12.200
So now let's just look
at a similar example,

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:12.850
but in reverse.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:15.100
Let's look at a
potential data exposure.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.070
So let's look at
that same order API.

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:19.710
Someone sent in an order
ID, and your intent

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:23.520
is that you want to share out
the amount and the status.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:25.560
Maybe this is a product
that's being ordered,

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.930
and you want to show here
that it's been shipped.

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.185
But you also have a note
field that you exposed.

00:21:30.185 --> 00:21:32.310
Maybe that note has some
very important information

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.805
that you want to share
with the requester.

00:21:34.805 --> 00:21:36.930
In this example, we've
shown that the note actually

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.540
contained, in the Comment
field, a credit card number,

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:41.910
and you did not
intend to expose that.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:43.770
So by applying that
same policy that we

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:47.130
saw before, but on the
outbound, on the responses

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:49.530
here, again, that
sensitive data we

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:52.230
can mask in line so that
the requester still gets

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:54.930
the amount, still gets
the status of shipped,

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.603
still technically gets the note.

00:21:56.603 --> 00:21:58.770
But that sensitive information
has now been redacted

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:01.403
or if you chose you could
tokenize it as well.

00:22:01.403 --> 00:22:02.820
So this is just
showing how we can

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:05.460
help prevent a
potential data exposure

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:09.670
by inspecting and masking
that data on the response.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:10.170
Great.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.960
And with that we're going
to pass it back to Prithpal

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.490
to go to a more in-depth demo.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:16.710
We're going to show
again how we can combine

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:20.280
the power of our enterprise
data protection controls

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:23.520
with DLP with the
enterprise admin

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.190
and developer controls for
the Apigee and Apigee Edge

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:27.480
extensions.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.280
PRITHPAL BHOGILL:
Excellent Thanks, Scott.

